  existing flow — there's no flow here to bolt onto. It presupposes four things this repo doesn't have: tasks as data, a
  source of change events, a notion of a user who "cares," and a delivery channel. So: questions → approaches →
  sectioned design → written spec → plan. No code until you approve a design.

  One scope flag before I start asking. The request names a notification subsystem, but a notification system with
  nothing to notify about is untestable. Realistically this is two pieces — (1) tasks that exist and change, (2)
  notifications about those changes — and only the second is what you asked for. I'd like to know which side of that
  line we're on before I spend questions on delivery semantics.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether [38;5;153mindex.html[39m is the real app or a stand-in. This changes everything downstream — if there's a real app
  with a real task store and real users elsewhere, the design must fit its stack, and "notification" can mean
  server-pushed. If this repo is genuinely all there is, there is no server and no second user, so "notify" can only
  mean in-page UI over local state, and we'd have to build enough of a task model to have changes worth notifying about.

  - [1mThis repo is it (greenfield)[0m — cheapest to start, but we design a task model too, and multi-user/push is off the
    table until a backend exists. Hard to undo: the storage choice.
  - [1mStand-in for a real app[0m — I'd need to see that codebase before designing anything; the notification design follows
    its stack and auth. Costs a round trip, avoids designing against a fiction.
  - [1mPrototype for a future real app[0m — we build client-only now but shape the event model so a server can take it over
    later. Costs a little indirection up front.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m tell me it's a stand-in if it is — designing a notification subsystem against an empty page
  produces a spec that won't survive contact with the real code.
